{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310848,"nonce":"OKNqnDXt/Xk4I/VmlUwg6oyTJ40=","title":"The MIR loop vectorizer has never executed; Stage 0 made it reachable, gated and observable, and found it would miscompile if simply ...","message":"**Stage 0 SHIPPED 825079e9.** Direction (user): Cranelift does NOT autovectorize\ndespite the common claim, so rayzor needs its own pass to hand it vector\ninstructions. Same for wasm. LLVM already does this — the pass is for the other\ntiers and must stay OFF the LLVM AOT path.\n\n**IT HAD NEVER RUN.** `LoopVectorizationPass` (~2000 lines) was registered only\nin the O3 pass list, and `CompilationConfig.optimization_level` is only ever set\nto 0/1/2 with no CLI override, so `_ =\u003e O3` in `pipeline.rs:2373` is unreachable.\n\n**Forced on, it declines every loop, for reasons unrelated to trip counts:**\n```\naddConst (runtime bound)   Loop contains function calls\naddFixed (bound 1024)      No suitable induction variable found\n```\nThe calls are `rayzor_update_call_frame_location` — the stack-trace marker\nhir_to_mir emits unconditionally, two per loop body.\n`strip_stack_trace_updates` deletes them, but runs in the tiered backend at\nCODEGEN time, long after the MIR pass pipeline. Pass ordering, not the\ntransform. Whitelisting that callee gets the runtime-bound loop into\n`vectorize_loop`, which then declines it honestly (no symbolic trip count).\n\n**IT WOULD MISCOMPILE IF SIMPLY ENABLED — this is why it is gated OFF.**\n`compute_access_stride` returns `1` (\"contiguous-by-default\") whenever\n`find_gep_producing` finds nothing (`vectorization.rs:558`). Every nue access is\n`Mem.loadF32(base + Usize.fromInt(i \u003c\u003c 2))`, which lowers to `shl`+`add` on i64\nand NEVER produces a GEP. So the pass would certify 100% of nue accesses as\nunit-stride with no evidence. Also broken: `create_epilogue_loop` never advances\nthe index between copies; `finalize_reduction` emits\n`VectorReduce { dest: acc, vector: acc }` (an SSA violation); the inline-`Phi`\nIV branch misidentifies reduction accumulators as IVs.\n\n**Instruments now in the tree:**\n- `RAYZOR_VECTORIZE=1` — opt in, at O2 and O3, default OFF.\n- `RAYZOR_VECTORIZE_LOG=1` — every loop considered, why declined, and the NAME\n  of the callee that blocked it. `failure_reason` always existed with no reader.\n- Cranelift `[COMPILE_FAIL]` is now unconditional: it installed a trap stub and\n  warned via tracing, so the build succeeded and the program died at exit 133\n  in silence. Every bug in a new pass would have presented as that.\n\n**MEASURED ORDERING CONSTRAINT:** the vectorizer must run BEFORE\n`ControlFlowSimplificationPass`. Run after, and `LoopNestInfo::analyze` finds\nZERO loops in code that plainly still loops — so the pass silently sees nothing.\n(This contradicts the intuition that simplifying first helps; do not \"fix\" it.)\n\n**NEXT (Stage 1, ~3-5 days), not started:** flip every optimistic default to\nreject; build an affine-address recognizer walking integer `Add/Sub/Shl/Mul`\nback to the IV or a loop-invariant root (replaces both `find_gep_producing` and\n`compute_access_stride`); rewrite the transform as clone-and-widen rather than\nin-place mutation. First element type i32 (no ulp argument). First real kernel =\nin-place unit-stride f32 scale, `FlashDecode.hx:378-382` and `:470-476`, where\nthe store address EQUALS the load address so no aliasing guard is needed.\nFor aliasing generally: a runtime overlap check with scalar fallback, NOT a\n`@:noalias` annotation and NOT static analysis — two `Usize` params are two\ni64s with no provenance, and this repo's own tests derive one base from another.\nFull plan: `scratchpad/vec_plan.md` (regenerable).\n\nRelated: [[perf_g1_gep_made_plain_haxe_loops_vectorise]],\n[[bugs_mir_local_scan_global_conclusion]], [[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_mir_vectorizer_stage0` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}